// Practice quiz: Checked vs unchecked exceptions
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/exceptions-and-logging/checked-vs-unchecked-exceptions

::o1 which is checked::Which of these exception classes is checked? {
~IllegalArgumentException#Not quite. It extends RuntimeException, so it is unchecked.
=IOException#Correct. IOException extends Exception but not RuntimeException, so the compiler checks it.
~ArithmeticException#Not quite. It extends RuntimeException, so it is unchecked.
~StackOverflowError#Not quite. It is an Error, and Errors are unchecked.
}

::o1 extends Exception::A class extends Exception but not RuntimeException. What kind of exception is it? {
~Unchecked, because every Exception is unchecked#Not quite. Only RuntimeException and its subclasses are unchecked among Exceptions.
~An Error#Not quite. Error is a separate branch under Throwable.
~Checked only when it is thrown from main#Not quite. Where it is thrown does not matter; the class hierarchy decides.
=Checked#Correct. Every Exception subclass outside RuntimeException is checked.
}

::o2 unreported sleep::A method calls Thread.sleep(100), which is declared to throw InterruptedException. The method has no try/catch and no throws clause. What happens? {
~It compiles and may throw at run time#Not quite. That is how unchecked exceptions behave; this one is checked.
~It compiles with a warning#Not quite. An unreported checked exception is an error, not a warning.
~It compiles because the JVM catches the exception#Not quite. The compiler checks this before the program ever runs.
=It does not compile, because InterruptedException must be caught or declared to be thrown#Correct. The compiler requires every checked exception to be handled or declared.
}

::o2 callers of throws::A method is declared with throws IOException. What must code that calls it do? {
=Catch IOException or declare throws IOException itself#Correct. Each caller handles it or passes it on by declaring it.
~Nothing, because the method already declared it#Not quite. Declaring passes the obligation to the caller; it does not remove it.
~Always wrap it in a RuntimeException#Not quite. Wrapping is one choice, but catching or declaring both satisfy the compiler.
~Catch it only when main is the caller#Not quite. The rule applies to every caller.
}

::o3 recoverable failure::A user types the name of a settings file that turns out not to exist, and the caller could ask for another name. What is the usual choice? {
~Signal it with an unchecked exception, because file problems are bugs#Not quite. A missing file is an expected condition, not a mistake in the code.
~Throw an Error#Not quite. Errors are for serious problems an application should not try to catch.
=Signal it with a checked exception#Correct. The caller can reasonably recover, so the compiler should make it plan for the failure.
~Return null and say nothing#Not quite. A null hides the failure, and a caller may forget to check for it.
}

::o3 programming bug::A method is called with a negative count, which only happens when the calling code has a bug. What is the usual choice? {
=Signal it with an unchecked exception such as IllegalArgumentException#Correct. A bug should be fixed in the caller, not caught by every caller.
~Signal it with a checked exception so every caller must catch it#Not quite. Forcing every caller to handle a bug adds code that cannot sensibly recover.
~Throw an Error#Not quite. Errors are for serious problems in the JVM, not bad arguments.
~Catch it inside the method and carry on with 0#Not quite. Hiding the bug lets wrong results spread.
}
